[Unit]
Description=Dashboard availability watchdog (hysteresis probe, storm escape)

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# 2026-07-30 incident. This ExecStart used to be a one-line inline shell: a
# 5-second HTTP fetch of the DEEP health endpoint, ORed with an unconditional
# `systemctl --user restart dark-factory-dashboard.service`. (Reproduced only
# in prose — the literals are asserted ABSENT from this file by
# tests/scripts/test_dashboard_watchdog.py::test_b6_unit_has_no_inline_shell_probe,
# so no future edit can paste the old line back under a comment marker and
# then uncomment it.)
#
# Two defects, compounding. It probed the DEEP health endpoint — three 5s DB
# probes — so a dashboard that was merely SLOW read as dead; and it restarted
# on a SINGLE miss with no rate ceiling, so every restart made the next probe
# likelier to time out. Measured result: 192 restarts in 3 hours, ~27%
# downtime, from a service that was still serving requests throughout.
#
# The probe therefore moved to the SHALLOW endpoint (/api/health — a bare
# {'status': 'ok'} handler with no DB access), and the policy moved out of
# this file entirely. Supervision logic inside an `sh -c` string is a
# contract-in-prose (INV-1): no test can reach it, which is how a
# single-sample restarter shipped unnoticed. It now lives in a real script
# with a real test suite:
#
#   policy    scripts/dashboard-watchdog.py  (hysteresis, grace, rate ceiling)
#   tests     tests/scripts/test_dashboard_watchdog.py
#             dashboard/tests/test_dashboard_watchdog_storm_escape.py
#   contract  plans/dashboard-availability-prd.md §Contract  ← the real one
#
# Type=oneshot is load-bearing: every timer tick is a FRESH process, so the
# failure streak and the rolling restart window are persisted to disk by the
# script rather than held in memory.
ExecStart=/home/leo/src/dark-factory/scripts/dashboard-watchdog.py
# The script logs its decisions via systemd-cat -t dashboard-watchdog; these
# two route anything else it prints to the same journal, so
# `journalctl --user -t dashboard-watchdog` is the single place to read why
# the watchdog did — or deliberately did not — act.
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
